theory.intro:
A **unit fraction** has a numerator of $1$. Finding a unit fraction of a quantity means sharing the quantity into that many equal parts.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"fractionBlueFill","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Identify the one selected fifth of five equal parts; restrained mathematical fraction shading."}],"reference":"Equal-part quantity bar; preserve outer geometry and ordinary black outlines/labels"}
\definecolor{fractionBlueFill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=0.9cm,every node/.style={font=\small}]
\fill[fractionBlueFill] (0,0) rectangle (1,1);
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5,1);
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4} {\draw (\x,0)--(\x,1);}
\node[above] at (0.5,1.05) {one part};
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=5pt}] (0,-0.15)--(5,-0.15);
\node[below] at (2.5,-0.35) {the whole quantity};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
Find $\frac{1}{4}$ of $28$.
[tikz]
% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[],"reference":"Equal-part quantity bar; preserve outer geometry and ordinary black outlines/labels"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=0.8cm]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (4,1);
\foreach \x in {1,2,3} {\draw (\x,0)--(\x,1);}
\node[above] at (2,1) {total $28$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

